cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use or build on this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Icy Ewe: 3D scanning on the cheap. A hobbyist's field guide and a defensive publication"
type: report
version: "1.1"
date-released: "2026-11-13"
license: CC-BY-SA-4.0
url: "https://github.com/The-Dorkknight/icy-ewe"
repository-code: "https://github.com/The-Dorkknight/scantastic"
authors:
  - family-names: Armstrong
    given-names: Matthew
    alias: The-Dorkknight
abstract: >-
  How Scantastic turns an Intel RealSense SR305 or D415 and a computer as slow as a 7th-gen Core i3 into a tabletop
  3D scanner: picture features that check every alignment step, a drift-bounded loop-closure rule, a one-axis
  turntable mode with an optional marker ring, live keyframe tracking, a crash-tolerant record-now-stitch-later mode
  for slow PCs, speed profiles with order-preserving parallel alignment, an exact cropped polish, a gated graphics-card
  path and a flat-wall sensor check. Includes the method in rebuildable detail, mistakes, results on made-up scans, a
  test plan, and a defensive disclosure of the methods and variations, dedicated to the public. The methods have not
  yet been run on a real camera.
keywords:
  - 3D scanning
  - depth camera
  - RealSense
  - RGB-D
  - ICP
  - loop closure
  - turntable
  - defensive publication
  - prior art
